nd out more to  seek out why and critically to explain  what they have found and its importance  professor gage is just that kind of  thinker and scholar she also happens to  be one of our most celebrated educators  receiving the Cyrillic off award for  teaching excellence in Yale College in  2009 most seriously though she happens  to be the mother of a very kind son who  happens to have as one of his best  friends my very kind son and together  they enjoy playing Minecraft playing  Super Smash Bros and a multitude of  other games I simply can't master ladies  and gentlemen a member of the Yale class  of 1990 for an American Studies major  who graduated magna [ __ ] laude and with  distinction and now an award-winning  historian who discovers Holy Grails  Beverly gage  well thank you Jonathan and thanks to  all of you for being here for yet one  more event in this heat thanks to all of  you the class of 2019  I should make no.1 note to what Jonathan  was saying about the Martin Luther King  letter that I discovered and wrote about  over the summer that I was actually not  the person who found the document that  it was actually my undergraduate  research assistant who happens to be  sitting here right now Jakob Wasserman  who was sitting there in the National  Archives taking photographs for me so  come Italy Jacob didn't know what he was  looking at but nonetheless he did take  the photograph of the document so I  realized in preparing this talk that  2015 marks the 25th anniversary of my  own start at Yale as a freshman I came  to Yale in 1990 so this was back in the  Dark Ages when there was a a smoking  section inside the library and be nobody  had an email account  realizing that 25 years have passed  since that moment of course makes me  feel a little bit old and I realize now  looking around that I am one of the few  people in the room who was even born in  1990 but it also makes me want to reach  back in time to my 18 year old self and  assure her that things were going to  turn out ok because that's not at all  how I felt when I was sitting here in  Woolsey Hall 25 years ago about to start  my first classes at Yale and that may or  may not be how all of you feel sitting  here today and some of the connections  between then and now or to put it  another way between there and here or  what I'm going to talk about today when  I arrived on the Yale campus 25 years  ago I immediately suspected that I was  not going to be a very good Yale student  I was a good student in the sense that  you are all presumably good students I  got good grades in high school and I  knew how to work hard when I had to  though I did not always necessarily work  as hard as I could and that was part of  being a good student too but I was not  sure that I would turn out to be a very  good Yale student and so let me explain  a bit about why that was from my first  moment on campus  I found myself confronted with what I've  come to think of as the three somewhat  contradictory messages that Yale  delivers to arriving freshmen the first  was a message of praise you alone among  thousands of talented wonderful  brilliant people who applied to Yale  have been selected for admission and  this means that each one of you is very  very special the second which you are  the second was a message of pressure you  are now surrounded by thousands of  talented wonderful brilliant people and  you had better step it up because this  isn't high school anymore and you're not  going to be the best one the third and I  think maybe the most difficult for me  was a message of privilege as the  talented brilliant wonderful person you  are you have been granted entry into one  of the world's wealthiest and most  exclusive clubs a place that will give  you access to global leaders and titans  of industry and famous artists and  writers and thinkers all because you are  now one of us frankly I did not know  what to think  about all of this and I came away from  my first days at Yale with a lot of  conflicting thoughts and emotions I was  excited to be here  thrilled about the opportunity that had  been handed to me and eager to meet my  classmates and my professors at the same  time I was nervous about the future  afraid that I would not live up to  expectations and wondering if maybe Yale  had a big blind spot when it came to its  own wealth and influence and privilege  so I did what I think many freshmen do  instead of plunging into campus life  and trying new things I started out  doing exactly what I knew I was good at  and I started out only doing those  things where I knew I could excel in my  case that was classical music I had  played the piano and the violin fairly  seriously in high school and I had done  some conducting and some singing and so  I started my life at Yale by signing up  for piano lessons and taking music  theory and joining chamber music  performance and that was that but  something curious happened over the  course of my freshman year here despite  all of my conflicted feelings despite  all of my worries I began to learn from  the people I encountered and from the  ideas that I was finding in class I  learned first of all that there was not  just one Yale that Yale was many  different things to many different  people and that this was actually the  whole point there were lots of people on  campus including I should mention the  Dean of the college and the president of  the University as you saw on Saturday  who had their own views about Yale's  strengths and weaknesses but who  nonetheless loved being here and sought  to make Yale the best place that it  could be I also learned that I was  fascinated by history and literature and  that despite my music centered schedule  those were the things that really got me  excited and so at the end of my freshman  year I gave my first ever and still only  solo piano recital in the je dining-hall  je  you're out there okay  and then I quit taking music lessons for  the rest of my life I came back as a  sophomore American Studies major and  began pursuing the ideas and subjects  that still define what I do today so  that was my freshman year I acknowledge  on one level that this is not a very  dramatic story  it can actually be summed up in three  words I switched majors but it didn't  feel nearly so simple at the time as I  was trying to make these decisions about  my own life and to sort out the  difference between what other people  expected of me and what I wanted for  myself it was a time when I often felt  lonely and confused and unsure about  what I should do next but it was also  exciting and more than that it was  entirely necessary a chance to break out  of old molds and try something new and  this is the message that I want you to  take away from these first few days on  campus Yale is above all an opportunity  to grow and learn and change and to  think seriously about who you are and  what you want freshman year is the time  that you get to start writing the story  of your own life  so now let's fast-forward 25 years I've  been back here at Yale teaching as a  professor of American history for more  than a decade and in that role I spend a  lot of time talking with students about  what I often described as the paradoxes  of history but about the blend of change  and continuity that makes up the human  past Dean Holloway and president  salivate talked about these themes on  Saturday in the context of Yale  and its relationship with slavery and  white supremacy today I would like to  encourage you to think about those  questions not as they apply to Yale but  as they apply to your own lives as a  professor here I teach a course on the  history of liberalism and conservatism  in the United States and we look in that  class about how those two terms change  over time and in some ways stay the same  and as part of that class I ask students  to write their political autobiographies  to think about why they hold the  political views that they do and to  imagine what larger forces in society  might have led them to this place this  is something we often do when we're  thinking about historical figures what  part is John Calhoun the man and what  part was simply the times in which he  lived  the region in which he was born his race  or religion or class or the political  views of his own family so I ask  students to turn that lens on themselves  and to think about forces outside of  their own individual ambition or  temperament or intellect that may have  shaped how they think about the world  and they end up writing about all sorts  of things some students write about  conversations around the dinner table  about their mother who made them listen  to NPR or their father who made them  watch Fox News others write about their  direct experiences say the difficulties  and rewards of being the only out gay  student in a conservative high school or  of being the quote/unquote scholarship  kid in a world of wealth sometimes since  this is Yale students write about  hanging out with their parents in the  White House  and watching politics unfold from the  top down  others write about their grassroots  experiences the thrills and  disappointments of working on a  political campaign or throwing their  energies into a movement for social  transformation so a lot of these papers  draw on early experiences elements of  childhood or even deeper back into  family history but one of the things  that has struck me most of all in  reading these papers has been how much  students change during their time at  Yale just for the record I don't mean  that they all become raging liberals  which is what many people in the outside  world would think actually happens  inside of Yale I mean that they and now  you change in all sorts of different  ways and find a vast range of new  experiences in the next four years some  of you who may run for local political  office or student government many of you  will probably vote for the first time  hopefully all of you will meet someone  who fundamentally challenges your  assumptions and either persuades you to  their cause or shows you how to  respectfully disagree some of you may  discover new aspects of your sexuality  or new extracurricular interests and  some of you may read a book or take a  class that significantly changes your  point of view whatever those experiences  may be the key is that they will happen  here that Yale changes and broadens and  transforms the people within its  community your job is to take all of  this in and then discard some of it  those parts that aren't useful or that  don't speak to you in meaningful ways  think of it as an editing process this  year you get to write a first draft of  your own Yale experience and  you have three more years to fine-tune  what you want to do and what you want to  say and at the end of it all you will  hopefully have a remarkable story of  your own to tell right the other thing  that professors do at Yale besides  teaching is that we conduct our own  research  well we actually also attend faculty  meetings and serve on committees but  since it's so hot in here I will not go  into elaborate descriptions of all of  that at the moment as Dean Holloway  mentioned my own research project is a  really big fat doorstop biography of j  edgar Hoover who was the director of the  FBI from 1924 to 1972 so a big chunk of  the 20th century 48 years often when I  tell people that I am writing a  biography of Hoover they say oh why  would you want to spend so much time  with such an awful man for those of you  who aren't familiar with Hoover's  reputation he's widely known as a kind  of tyrannical figure in Washington so  someone who was spying on everyone and  then using all of that information to  kind of cement his own political power  which is how he lasted 48 years in  office part of my task as a biographer  is to attempt to humanize this man and  to make him into more than just a  one-dimensional villain one of the ways  that I've tried to do this is to take  another look at his childhood and  especially his college years and to  think about how those early experiences  shaped the person he became for instance  he was born and bred in Washington DC in  a little house that was just five blocks  from the Capitol so he grew up in a  culture of government service  his father was mentally ill with what we  would today describe as something like  severe depression  so Hoover never had the chance to go  away to college and he attended night  school at home in Washington so that he  could stay nearby and earn money and  take care of his parents during college  he joined a fraternity called Kappa  Alpha which was a southern fraternity  founded explicitly to carry on  traditions of white supremacy and to  promote the values of the antebellum  slaveholding south so this was early  training in a kind of racialized  thinking that would ultimately have very  serious consequences for the entire  nation and then he happened to graduate  from college in 1917 at the very moment  that the United States was entering  World War one and so he stepped into a  government job because he was a boy in  Washington and the bureaucracy was  expanding and as it turned out he never  left he went to work at the Justice  Department at the age of 22 became head  of the FBI at 29 and died in that same  job at the age of 77 I tell this story  not to suggest that the first job you  take out of college will be the job that  you will have to hold for the rest of  your life instead I want to emphasize  that we are all of us even supposed  one-dimensional tyrants like Hoover at  once the products of the world into  which we are born or the organizations  we join in college and our own  individual ambitions and desires and  we'll there are already many factors  that have combined to put each of you on  your current path in life and there will  be many other factors often beyond your  control that shape where you end up  right now at this plastic moment in time  the day before your first  to college classes this is a moment to  sit back and reflect about what you want  and how you want to shape your own path  into the future  okay so now as I finish up I also want  to offer a few words of practical advice  I know you've been getting a lot of this  in recent days particularly before you  plunge into the strange and wonderful  experience known as Yale shopping period  all right point one don't go crazy there  are more than a thousand classes being  offered this semester at Yale and the  purpose of shopping period is not to  shock them all shopping period is really  great to try out classes where you think  you might be interested it's a wonderful  opportunity to experiment but it can  sometimes make people a little crazy if  they're running from class to class to  class so be selective about what you do  in the next few days leave room in your  schedule for something that you're doing  just because you want to do it but don't  go around to every single class because  the truth of the matter is that you  actually can't know what you're going to  get out of class until you've invested  something of yourself in it and that  process doesn't really begin until  shopping period is over  point to be selfish on Saturday Dean  Holloway asked you to thank your parents  and your teachers and the people from  your hometown for all they've done for  you all of which is wonderful but now  they are gone and I can offer a  different piece of advice which is to  forget about them don't really do that  entirely but make the choices that you  ultimately want to make here on campus  because you are the only one who is  living here and you are the only one who  is experiencing your own life number  three ask for help there's a lot you  still  no despite this week of activity about  how Yale works and that is how it should  be even if it seems like everyone around  you already knows everything I can tell  you with 100% certainty that they are  faking it so don't be afraid to ask even  basic information and for instance it  took me a long time as a freshman to  figure out the professor's really want  you to go to their office hours that  they hold office hours so you can stop  by they can get to know you and you can  have a real intellectual and personal  exchange I have one other little piece  of gathering information advice and I am  going to abuse my place up here on the  platform and Dean Holloway can't object  because he is also a historian in the  history department tomorrow night in the  history department we are holding a  shopping party for all undergraduates  who are interested in history to come  get to know us learn about our classes  meet new professors it's actually pretty  fun and you should all come to that  seven-day 30 HGS 2:11 I'm hosting ok you  should all come so even if history is  not your thing  you should get out there and explore  everything that's on offer in these next  couple of weeks as this information is  being offered to you and that includes  point number 4 there are only six in  total point number 4 explore New Haven  Yale can sometimes be a bubble or to put  it less charitably a fortress with big  iron gates that mark the difference  between being inside and being outside  but the city of New Haven is a place  that has a lot to teach all of you and  it's a city where your energies can  really make a difference when I was an  undergraduate I worked in New Haven as a  GED teacher I taught writing classes in  a women's prison and I interned at a  local newspaper and though I loved my  Yale class  is these other places taught me a great  deal as well  number five quit things this was what I  did and it was really great it was  absolutely the right choice or at least  quit the right things the ones that  aren't truly engaging your energies and  finally take care of yourself and I mean  this in two ways the first is the more  literal Yale tends to push people to do  more and faster and better to stay up  all night and then brag about how little  you slept and then wake up and sign up  for even more this is actually not a  good idea  the bigger challenge here is to find  some sort of balance in your life  to make sure that you have time to do  things like eat and sleep and read and  reflect and sometimes do nothing at all  so that's one way that I hope that you  will all take care of yourselves but I  also hope that during your four years  here you'll find a way to take care of  yourself that part of you that is more  than the sum total of accomplishments  and expectations and who you might  appear to be on paper these next four  years are a time to explore new ideas  and be remarkable new people and to  challenge yourselves in new ways part of  this means figuring out who you are and  who you want to be and what you want to  contribute to the world your four years  at Yale starting tomorrow are a chance  to throw out the old script and to write  your own story of your own life thank  you  this is a place as you're writing your  own life this is a place that you  discover you will discover has many  components to it that claim at least to  be timeless one of these is the song  bright college years known also as our  alma mater the glee club will help you  learn the alma mater this is a song that  that's your cue to come up are you guys  coming up on stage this is very well  rehearsed this is a song that they will  help you learn today and it will also be  a song you'll hear numerous times that  your Yale career and it'll be the last  song you sing together in May of 2019  even though I was not myself an  undergraduate here her the song quite a  bit in my 8 years of living in the  college the song strikes a deep chord  with me and I think it's actually quite  true the lyrics that that is hopefully  it will come to mean as much to you as  it does to this guy who didn't even come  to this school  the choir the club skews a Yale glee  club is going to sing the lyrics on the  back by the way he'll sing the first  verse by themselves they'll repeat that  first verse which you will then join and  reading the second verse the one last  piece of advice or direction on your  seats I believe you all have or you're  given this Yale 19 handkerchief I  actually don't know the history behind  this one it's charming well it's weird  the first time you see it then becomes  charming after a while in the closing  lines of into the second verse you'll  see for God for country and for Yale you  will take this handkerchief in your  right one left hand but we know in the  hand and you will go for God for country  and for Yale and you will wonder why and  you will feel awkward but there are a  thousand other people feeling awkward  with you at the same time it's your  first lesson in togetherness  ladies and gentlemen yell glee club  Jeffrey Douma in the Yale glee club  thank you very much you are now excused  wait don't move Hana  where's Hana you said you'd be in the is  there an order of excellent exit the  same way you came in enjoy your evening  have a lovely time at freshman dinner  and have a fabulous time in class  tomorrow
 

